# .otto — otto-factory project config
#
# This file belongs in YOUR PROJECT folder (e.g. ~/my-project/.otto),
# not inside the otto-factory folder. Copy it there and fill in the values:
#
#   cp ~/otto-factory/.otto.example ~/my-project/.otto
#
# Otto-factory reads this file automatically when you run commands from
# your project directory. You do not need to reference it in any command.
#
# Lines starting with # are comments — they are ignored and are here to
# explain what each setting does. Only the lines without # are active.


# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Your project's GitHub repository.
#
# Format: your-github-username/your-repo-name
# Example: alice/my-first-project
#
# You can find your username at github.com — it appears in the top-right
# corner when you are signed in. The repo name must match exactly what
# appears on GitHub (it is case-sensitive).
#
# Full URLs also work:
#   GITHUB_REPO=https://github.com/alice/my-first-project
#
GITHUB_REPO=your-username/your-repo-name


# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Shell command to run your test suite after each coding attempt.
#
# After the coder writes new code, otto-factory runs this command to check
# whether the tests pass. If they fail, the output is sent back to the coder
# as feedback so it can fix the problem before opening a Pull Request.
#
# Leave this blank if your project has no automated tests — that is fine,
# especially for simple projects or when just getting started.
#
# Examples:
#   TEST_COMMAND=npm test          ← JavaScript / Node.js
#   TEST_COMMAND=pytest            ← Python
#   TEST_COMMAND=go test ./...     ← Go
#   TEST_COMMAND=cargo test        ← Rust
#
# Note: if your runtime (node, python, etc.) is installed via a version
# manager like nvm or pyenv, you may also need to set SHELL_INIT in
# otto-factory/.env. See the Configuration reference in the README.
#
TEST_COMMAND=

# The folder the coder stages with `git add` before committing.
#
# When the coder finishes writing files, it runs `git add <SOURCE_GLOB>`
# to stage them. This setting controls which folder gets staged.
#
# Use "src" (the default) if all your code lives in a src/ subfolder.
# Use "." if your project files are at the root level with no src/ folder.
#
# If you are unsure, look at where your project's main files are:
#   - Files at ~/my-project/index.html, ~/my-project/app.js → use SOURCE_GLOB=.
#   - Files at ~/my-project/src/index.html, ~/my-project/src/app.js → use SOURCE_GLOB=src
#
SOURCE_GLOB=src
